# Executing specifications whose streams carry Tau specifications.
#
# See demos/README.md for how to run this script.

# demo_3.1 ran specifications over sbf streams, whose values are simple Boolean
# functions. This one uses streams of type tau, so the values flowing through
# them are Tau specifications: the programs read and written are themselves
# programs.

i1:tau := in console.
o1:tau := out console.

# To start, a specification that does not look at its input at all. Its output at
# every step is a fixed Tau specification, given as a constant of the Tau Boolean
# algebra -- the braces of demo_1.1. As in demo_3.1, the run advances at the
# continue prompt on anything but q, which here is a `# step` comment:

r o1[t] = { o1[t] = i1[t] }
# step
# step
q

# Now a specification that does read its input. Since a value here is a whole
# specification, it is typed the way you would write one, ending in a period --
# unlike the sbf values of demo_3.1, which take no period.

# This program echoes its input. Note that the value entered below is a
# specification with no `always` or `sometimes`, so it is implicitly an `always`
# statement, and the interpreter prints it back with the `always` made explicit.

# Note also that the run is constrained only at time point 0. A specification
# asserted at every time point would go on asking for input forever and swallow
# the rest of this script, so each run below is deliberately bounded.

r o1[0] = i1[0]
o1[t] = 0.
q

# Next, a program that complements its input rather than echoing it. Complement in
# the Tau Boolean algebra is negation of the specification, so feeding it
# `always o1[t] = 0` yields the dual, `sometimes o1[t] != 0`:

r o1[0] = i1[0]'
always o1[t] = 0.
q

# Note that this is why the term operator ' is used rather than the formula
# operator !: the input stream value is a term of the Tau Boolean algebra, not a
# formula.

# Finally, down the rabbit hole. The specification fed in below is itself one that
# reads and writes specifications, so complementing it produces a specification
# about specifications about specifications:

r o1[0] = i1[0]'
sometimes o1[t] = { o1[t] = i1[t] }.
q

# The nesting has no fixed depth. A Tau specification is an element of a Boolean
# algebra like any other, so it can be a value carried on a stream, and a
# specification reasoning about those values is again just a Tau specification.

quit
